---
/**
 * The scoring explainer.
 *
 * Every number the lesson studio shows is derived from one of four formulas.
 * Documenting them in one place keeps the drill honest: the same formulas run
 * in `src/lib/typing/scoring.ts`, and the notes below are the user-facing
 * version of that file.
 *
 * Render this inside a ContentSection (it starts at paragraph level, with no
 * heading of its own).
 */
---

<p>
	Four numbers come out of a run, and they answer different questions. Speed asks how fast you typed;
	accuracy asks how much of it was right; keystroke accuracy also counts the mistakes you corrected.
	The lesson studio never mixes them up.
</p>

<dl class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
	<div class="rounded-card border border-border bg-surface p-4">
		<dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Gross WPM</dt>
		<dd class="mt-1.5 text-sm">
			<code class="block font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">(characters ÷ 5) ÷ minutes</code>
			<span class="mt-1.5 block">
				Every five characters count as one standard word, so 300 characters in one minute is 60 gross
				WPM. This is the raw speed of the hands, before mistakes are taken off.
			</span>
		</dd>
	</div>

	<div class="rounded-card border border-border bg-surface p-4">
		<dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Net WPM</dt>
		<dd class="mt-1.5 text-sm">
			<code class="block font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">((characters − errors) ÷ 5) ÷ minutes</code>
			<span class="mt-1.5 block">
				The same calculation with the wrong characters removed from the count. This is the number the
				lesson panel shows during a timed drill, and the one used for a personal best.
			</span>
		</dd>
	</div>

	<div class="rounded-card border border-border bg-surface p-4">
		<dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Character accuracy</dt>
		<dd class="mt-1.5 text-sm">
			<code class="block font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">
				((characters − errors) ÷ characters) × 100
			</code>
			<span class="mt-1.5 block">
				The final state of the passage: a character that was wrong and then fixed with Backspace is
				correct again, so it does not count against character accuracy. Characters still wrong when
				the run ends do. A lesson passes or fails on this number.
			</span>
		</dd>
	</div>

	<div class="rounded-card border border-border bg-surface p-4">
		<dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Keystroke accuracy</dt>
		<dd class="mt-1.5 text-sm">
			<code class="block font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">(correct keystrokes ÷ all keystrokes) × 100</code>
			<span class="mt-1.5 block">
				Every character you press, including the ones you delete again, so this is the stricter
				measure: a run that was corrected five times can be 100% character-accurate and still below
				100% keystroke accuracy. The per-key analysis uses the same raw keystrokes.
			</span>
		</dd>
	</div>
</dl>

<div class="space-y-2 text-sm">
	<p>
		<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Time starts on the first keystroke.</strong> Reading the
		passage before you begin is free, and an empty run never divides by zero: speed is reported as
		<code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">0</code> rather than
		<code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">NaN</code> or
		<code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink">Infinity</code>.
	</p>
	<p>
		<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Paused time is removed.</strong> While a drill is paused the
		clock stops, and the paused milliseconds are subtracted from the minutes in every formula. Pausing
		cannot inflate a WPM, and resuming does not throw away the text you had already typed.
	</p>
	<p>
		<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Short drills do not show live speed.</strong> A 100-character
		sequence is not a speed measurement, so lessons 1–9 show elapsed time and accuracy while you type,
		then report net WPM once the passage is finished. Lesson 10 is the timed 60-second paragraph, so it
		shows live WPM.
	</p>
</div>
